    // magnitude on a real heavy account, so on total work this index loses; it is kept because the
    // cost lands on a background write and the saving lands on a user-facing open.
    //
    // Optimizer boundary (MySQL): index selection is a cost-model decision, not a guarantee. At the
    // row widths above, MySQL 8.0.46 chose this index with no sort at every selectivity measured
    // from 0.5% to 90%. On a slim-row fixture an earlier measurement saw MySQL abandon it between
    // 60% and 70% in favour of `[accountId, archivedAt]` plus a sort over the whole account. That
    // degradation is graceful — the fallback is exactly the plan this index does not exist for — and
    // `FORCE INDEX` recovered ~28x there, so the boundary is a documented limit, not a hazard.
    //
    // SQLite statistics: nothing in `apps/server` runs `ANALYZE` or `PRAGMA optimize`, so a shipped
    // light/self-hosted database has no `sqlite_stat1`. That is not load-bearing: on the real schema
